name: verify-live-mostlylucid
description: >
  The only script in this directory that asserts on real articles from a real
  publisher.

  Everything else either runs against the seeded fixture (reserved .test
  addresses that cannot resolve) or, like verify-mostlylucid.yaml, stops at
  the point where discovery says it found something. Neither of them can tell
  you that a refresh actually produced readable articles, which is the one
  question a reader has to be able to answer.

  So this subscribes to https://www.mostlylucid.net for real, waits for the
  fetch and the offline download, and then asserts four things about what
  came back: the item list is not empty, a row has a real title, the article
  renders with the offline badge gone (which is only true when the download
  completed AND StyloExtract produced extracted content, not a stub), and a
  full-text search for a word taken from a real article finds it.

  mostlylucid.net is the maintainer's own site and is the only host sanctioned
  for repeated automated fetching. No other publisher appears here, and this
  script must not grow one.

  Run it through ux-scripts/run-live-mostlylucid.sh. That script builds the
  throwaway profile this runs against, deletes it on the way out including on
  failure and on interrupt, and is what substitutes __SEARCH_WORD__ below -
  which is why this file is a template rather than a script. The word cannot
  be written down in advance: it has to come from an article that is on the
  site today, or "search finds a real word" would be a search for a word this
  file made up.
default_delay: 250

actions:
  - type: Wait
    value: "2500"
    description: Startup and the first tree load, against an empty profile

  - type: Screenshot
    value: 00-empty
    description: >
      An empty reader. Worth capturing: this profile is seeded with a
      settings.json before launch, so the first-run default feeds must NOT
      have fired here.

  # Selected first, because nothing is selected at startup and the status
  # line is only written by a query. Without the click the assertion below
  # would be reading an empty string and would pass for the wrong reason.
  - type: MouseDown
    target: "inside(name=SidebarSections) text='All items'"
    button: left
  - type: MouseUp
    target: "inside(name=SidebarSections) text='All items'"
    button: left
  - type: Wait
    value: "900"

  - type: Expect
    target: StatusText
    matcher: HasText
    value: "No articles here yet."
    description: >
      The first-run default feeds must not have fired against this profile:
      it has a settings.json, which FirstRunSeedPolicy treats as a profile
      that has been used before.

  - type: Expect
    target: ItemList
    matcher: HasCount
    value: "0"

  # =====================================================================
  # Subscribe, for real.
  # =====================================================================
  - type: Click
    target: AddFeedButton

  - type: Wait
    value: "800"

  - type: TypeText
    target: FeedUrlBox
    window_id: "Add Feed"
    value: "mostlylucid.net"

  - type: Click
    target: FindButton
    window_id: "Add Feed"

  - type: Expect
    target: DiscoveryStatusText
    window_id: "Add Feed"
    matcher: ContainsText
    value: "Found"
    timeout: 30000

  - type: Screenshot
    value: 01-discovered
    window_id: "Add Feed"

  - type: Click
    target: AddButton
    window_id: "Add Feed"
    description: >
      Subscribes to whatever discovery left ticked. For this site that is one
      feed: mostlylucid.net publishes its posts as both RSS and Atom, and
      discovery now recognises the second as the same articles in another
      format and leaves it unticked. The counts below are lower bounds
      regardless, since how many posts the site carries today is its own
      business; run-live-dedupe.sh is the script that asserts on the exact
      numbers.

  - type: Wait
    value: "4000"
    description: The writes, the sidebar reload and the start of the fetch

  # =====================================================================
  # Real articles arrive.
  #
  # The wait is long because three things have to happen in order: the feed
  # fetch, the parse and upsert, and then the offline download of the first
  # article's full text. Expect polls rather than sleeping, so the timeouts
  # below are ceilings and not costs.
  # =====================================================================
  - type: Wait
    value: "14000"
    description: Let the fetch, the parse and the first downloads land

  # Away and back, not All items twice.
  #
  # All items is still the selected row from the top of this script, and
  # SelectedFeedNode's setter returns early when the value has not changed,
  # so clicking it again reloads nothing and leaves whatever the Add wrote on
  # the status line. Selecting Unread first makes the return to All items a
  # real selection change, which is what runs the query the assertion below
  # reads.
  - type: MouseDown
    target: "inside(name=SidebarSections) text='Unread'"
    button: left
  - type: MouseUp
    target: "inside(name=SidebarSections) text='Unread'"
    button: left
  - type: Wait
    value: "900"

  - type: MouseDown
    target: "inside(name=SidebarSections) text='All items'"
    button: left
  - type: MouseUp
    target: "inside(name=SidebarSections) text='All items'"
    button: left
  - type: Wait
    value: "1500"

  - type: Expect
    target: StatusText
    matcher: MatchesRegex
    value: "^[1-9][0-9]* articles$"
    timeout: 30000
    description: >
      A lower bound, not a count. How many articles mostlylucid.net is
      carrying today is the site's business; that there is at least one is
      this script's.

  - type: Screenshot
    value: 02-real-articles

  # =====================================================================
  # A row has a real title, and opening it renders a real body.
  #
  # J rather than a click on a row: no article title can be written down in
  # advance here, and J is the next-item shortcut, which selects the first
  # row when nothing is selected. It also gets the bare-letter shortcut
  # exercised against live data on the way past.
  # =====================================================================
  - type: PressKey
    target: ItemList
    value: "J"

  - type: Wait
    value: "2000"

  - type: Expect
    target: ArticleTitleText
    matcher: MatchesRegex
    value: "^.{8,}$"
    description: A real headline, not "Untitled" and not empty

  - type: Expect
    target: ArticleMetaText
    matcher: ContainsText
    value: "mostlylucid"
    description: The feed name the article actually came from

  - type: Expect
    target: OfflineBadge
    matcher: IsHidden
    timeout: 90000
    description: >
      The strongest assertion in this file. The badge is hidden on exactly one
      combination, Downloaded plus Extracted, which means the article page was
      fetched and HtmlToMarkdownService (StyloExtract) produced extracted
      content for it. A stub, an empty body or a failed fetch all leave the
      badge showing with wording of their own.

  - type: Screenshot
    value: 03-real-article
    description: The reading pane, rendering a real mostlylucid.net article

  # =====================================================================
  # The publisher's own categories arrived as tags.
  #
  # Every post on mostlylucid.net carries several <category> elements, and
  # nothing in this run typed a tag: the profile is new, the fixture is not
  # loaded, and no action above touched the tag strip. So a chip on this
  # article and a Tags section in the sidebar can only have come from the
  # feed.
  #
  # Lower bounds again, and for the same reason the article count is one:
  # which categories the site files a post under today is the site's
  # business. What is asserted is that some arrived, not which. The names
  # that actually landed are printed by run-live-mostlylucid.sh.
  # =====================================================================
  - type: Expect
    target: "first(name=RemoveTagButton)"
    matcher: IsVisible
    timeout: 20000
    description: >
      At least one tag chip on an article nobody tagged, which is the
      publisher's category having been imported when the item was stored.

  - type: Expect
    target: SidebarSections
    matcher: HasCount
    value: "3"
    description: >
      Favourites, Feeds and Tags. The section only exists when something is
      tagged, and nothing in this run tagged anything by hand.

  - type: Expect
    target: AddTagButton
    matcher: HasText
    value: "+"
    description: >
      And the add control is still the collapsed "+" beside them: the chips
      here were not typed into it.

  - type: Screenshot
    value: 03b-publisher-tags
    description: >
      The imported tags: chips under the byline, and the Tags section in the
      sidebar built from them.

  # =====================================================================
  # Full-text search finds a word from a real article. The word is
  # substituted by run-live-mostlylucid.sh from the live feed.
  # =====================================================================
  - type: TypeText
    target: SearchBox
    value: "__SEARCH_WORD__"

  - type: Wait
    value: "2500"
    description: The 250ms debounce plus the FTS query

  - type: Expect
    target: StatusText
    matcher: MatchesRegex
    value: "^[1-9][0-9]* results? for"
    timeout: 20000

  - type: Screenshot
    value: 04-search-hit
